          "section:Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them"
        ],
        "exercise": "wrap-report",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "throw-in-finally",
        "says": "An exception thrown from finally replaces the one that was propagating.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Edge Cases and Their Handling"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "the page defers it to 3.1.3, whose practices cover finally and try-with-resources"
      },
      {
        "id": "throw-in-switch",
        "says": "A switch expression can use throw as the body of a case to reject an unexpected value.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Relevant Java Features",
          "example:12-custom-exception/README_3.2.2.md:5"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "Throwing from a switch expression's case is a way of writing the check; an if/else that throws passes the same tests, so level-parser grades the exception, its message and the null case instead."
      },
      {
        "id": "throw-null",
        "says": "Throwing a null reference throws a NullPointerException instead.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Key Points to Remember"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "a corner of the language no realistic task leads to; the quiz's two questions go to the throws clause and to unreachable code"
      },
      {
        "id": "throw-stops",
        "says": "A throw ends the method at once, so no statement after it runs.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Concept Explanation",
          "example:12-custom-exception/README_3.2.2.md:1"
        ],
        "exercise": "fail-fast",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "throws-for-unchecked",
        "says": "Listing an unchecked exception in throws documents it, but the compiler enforces nothing.",
        "basis": [
          "section:`throw` and `throws`"
        ],
        "exercise": "quiz",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "trace-at-creation",
        "says": "The stack trace is captured where the exception is created, so it is created at the throw.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Key Points to Remember",
          "section:Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them"
        ],
        "exercise": "fresh-exception",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "uncaught",
        "says": "An exception nobody catches ends the thread, and the default handler prints its trace.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Concept Explanation"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "observing a thread die needs a second thread and its uncaught-exception handler, which a single-threaded JUnit practice cannot show without timing"
      },
      {
        "id": "unreachable-after-throw",
        "says": "A statement directly after a throw in the same block is unreachable and does not compile.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Key Points to Remember"
        ],
        "exercise": "quiz",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "wrap-with-cause",
        "says": "When a catch block throws a new exception, the caught one is passed as its cause.",
